INT. STARLITE DRIVE-IN - PROJECTION BOOTH - NIGHT

A cramped concrete room above the snack bar. Two ancient projectors, cold. Film cans stacked like pizza boxes. A card table lit by a bare bulb. Through the little window: an empty lot of speaker posts, rusting under a half moon.

Five men and women, one deck of cards.

LENNY SZABO, 50s, hawk-nosed, camel-hair coat, deals. DOLORES CRUZ, 40s, silver hoops, poker face, sits with her back to the wall. RAFAEL "BIG RAFA" ORTEGA, 30s, enormous, patient, sits by the door. TIM FAIRWEATHER, 20s, sweaty, borrowed suit, a stack of chips he does not own. OLD KOSTAS, 70, knit cap, silent, sipping a paper cup of ouzo.

The pot in the middle is mostly cash. A little too much.

LENNY
Tim. Your bet.

TIM
Give me a second.

DOLORES
You've had a second.

TIM
I'm thinking.

KOSTAS
He is sweating.

TIM
It's hot in here.

RAFA
It's forty degrees.

TIM
Hot for me.

LENNY
Tim, bet or fold.

TIM
Raise. Five hundred.

DOLORES
With whose money?

TIM
Mine.

LENNY
Your money was in your uncle's safe, which is now a hole in a wall.

TIM
I don't know what you mean.

RAFA
Tim.

TIM
I don't know.

KOSTAS
Dolores. Count the chips.

Dolores stacks his chips with long fingers. Counts under her breath. Looks up.

DOLORES
Two thousand, one hundred.

LENNY
Exactly what was in the safe.

Silence. The projector fans tick as they cool. Rafa shifts his weight against the door. Tim looks at the window.

TIM
Okay, listen.

LENNY
Sit.

TIM
It was a loan. I was going to put it back.

DOLORES
With what?

TIM
With this pot.

KOSTAS
(laughing, dry)
He was going to win it back from the man he stole it from.

TIM
Mr. Szabo, I'm sorry. Honestly.

LENNY
Sorry is a word. Does it have a face?

TIM
No.

LENNY
Then it can't pay.

Rafa clicks the lock on the door. Dolores slides her hand into her bag. Kostas lifts his cup, drinks, and sets it down very carefully.

TIM
Wait. Wait. I can fix it.

RAFA
Sit down, Tim.

TIM
No, no, no.

He lunges for the window. Rafa's arm catches him by the collar. The card table goes over. Chips and cash scatter across the concrete.

DOLORES
Rafa!

LENNY
Let him go.

RAFA
Boss?

LENNY
Let him go. He has nowhere to run.

Rafa lets go. Tim crumples against the film cans. Lenny crouches beside him, collects a hundred-dollar bill from the floor, folds it neatly.

LENNY (CONT'D)
(gently)
Tim. What's in the cans?

TIM
Movies.

LENNY
Real movies?

TIM
(small)
Some.

Lenny opens a can. Inside, instead of film: bricks of cash, banded. Dolores whistles. Kostas lights a cigarette.

LENNY
Oh, Tim.

The booth door shudders under a kick from outside. Rafa draws. Dolores kills the bulb. Darkness.

Gunfire stitches the room from end to end.
